      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>How cold should the water be?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>For most benefits, 50-55&deg;F is the sweet spot. Going colder (down to 39&deg;F) doesn't clearly increase benefits and raises hypothermia risk. If you're new, start at 55&deg;F and work down only if you want to.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>How long should I stay in?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>1-3 minutes for beginners, 3-5 minutes for experienced plungers. There's no evidence that staying in longer than 5 minutes adds benefit, and the risk of hypothermia increases significantly after 10 minutes.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>How often should I cold plunge?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>2-4 times per week is enough for most benefits. Daily plunging is fine if you enjoy it, but the recovery benefits plateau. If you're strength training, avoid plunging immediately after workouts (it may blunt muscle adaptation).</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Can I cold plunge if I have high blood pressure?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>Cold exposure causes a transient spike in blood pressure and heart rate. If you have cardiovascular disease, hypertension, or are pregnant, consult your physician before starting cold water immersion.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Is a cold shower as good as a cold plunge?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>Cold showers provide some of the same benefits (mood, alertness, basic cold tolerance) but the water temperature is warmer (typically 50-70&deg;F) and the full-body immersion effect is less. For recovery benefits, a proper plunge at 50&deg;F is more effective.</p></div>
      </details>

